WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.459
Model selection starts with the task.

00:00:02.459 --> 00:00:07.962
A model used for chat assistance may not be the right model for embeddings,

00:00:07.962 --> 00:00:10.713
code generation, summarization, extraction, or structured classification.

00:00:10.713 --> 00:00:14.644
Teams should consider output quality, latency, licensing, context length, cost,

00:00:14.644 --> 00:00:18.575
privacy requirements, hardware fit, and support in the chosen runtime.

00:00:18.575 --> 00:00:24.471
The best model is the one that meets the use case under real operating constraints.

00:00:24.471 --> 00:00:29.581
Runtimes differ in the model formats they support, the API patterns they expose,

00:00:29.581 --> 00:00:31.940
and the hardware they use well.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:36.264
Some local runtimes provide OpenAI-compatible API shapes at a high level,

00:00:36.264 --> 00:00:38.622
which can make application integration easier.

00:00:38.622 --> 00:00:44.912
GGUF and other model packaging concepts matter because the runtime has to understand the model file,

00:00:44.912 --> 00:00:46.484
tokenizer, architecture, and quantization.

00:00:46.484 --> 00:00:50.415
Compatibility is a product of file, runtime, settings, and hardware.

00:00:50.415 --> 00:00:53.167
Execution may be CPU-only, GPU-accelerated, or hybrid.

00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:57.098
CPU-only serving can be simple but slow for larger models.

00:00:57.098 --> 00:01:01.421
GPU acceleration can improve performance but introduces VRAM constraints, driver dependencies,

00:01:01.421 --> 00:01:02.601
and scheduling concerns.

00:01:02.601 --> 00:01:06.139
Hybrid execution may split work across CPU and GPU,

00:01:06.139 --> 00:01:09.676
which can be practical on consumer or workstation hardware.

00:01:09.676 --> 00:01:14.393
Context length, batch size, concurrency, and memory tradeoffs all shape the result.

00:01:14.393 --> 00:01:16.752
Document working model and runtime combinations.

00:01:16.752 --> 00:01:21.076
Record model source, version, license, quantization or precision, runtime version, hardware,

00:01:21.076 --> 00:01:23.827
context settings, concurrency assumptions, and measured behavior.

00:01:23.827 --> 00:01:28.151
This helps reproduce a stable service, compare variants fairly, troubleshoot regressions,

00:01:28.151 --> 00:01:31.296
and explain why a particular configuration was selected.

00:01:31.296 --> 00:01:35.620
Without documentation, model serving becomes a fragile collection of lucky settings.
